parity_is_partial:
DECLARED LIMITATION, read this before trusting a green run. The golden file publishes one mode per circuit, the dominant one, and publishes no fs column at all. So this fixture can prove:
  - that the dominant mode the reference picked is in our mode set, with the same layer and hop count (DMidx);
  - that its elevation (DMele, and the path's own ele, which is the same number on every one of these 14 cases), its basic MUF (DMBMUF) and, below 4000 km, its section 5.1 reflection height (DMhr) agree;
  - that the mode set ends where the reference's does, through ptick, the equation (19) virtual slant range of the last mode the reference's own loop computed.
It cannot prove:
  - the E-layer screening frequency fs of any mode, because the oracle never prints one. No case in this corpus is screened, so the whole screening branch is unmeasured against the reference. The three screening_cases below are hand-computed from the published equations to cover it, and they are NOT reference-derived;
  - the other five F2 modes and the three E modes of each circuit, whose elevations and basic MUFs the oracle does not publish. They are exercised only indirectly: ptick is the last of them, so an error in the mode set's upper end moves it;
  - DMFprob, which is carried in every case and asserted in none. Equations (9) and (10) need the deltal and deltau decile factors of ITU-R P.1239 Tables 2 and 3, which are issue #1102 and are not in this tree. Inventing them to make a column green would be worse than leaving it unasserted.
  - the product elevation, elevationDeg, and the product slant range, virtualSlantRangeKm. Those are equation (13) and equation (19) at the section 5.1 height, which is what section 5.1 says equation (13)'s hr is and what the reference does not do (modeSet.ts deviation 2). The oracle cannot measure them, only disagree with them, so their per-case difference from ele/DMele and ptick is recorded in reference_divergence as a DIVERGENCE with a wide bound, never as parity.
